WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.500
Good morning. This is OmniView, episode 126. It's July 28th, 2026, and as always, we're covering

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.220
what happened from every angle. Let's look at the day's news from every angle.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.900
U.S. states now shoulder rising costs for food assistance, creating direct pressure to trim

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:26.400
recipient roles. The one big beautiful bill act altered federal food stamp funding so states

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.900
cover a larger share of program costs. This change gives states a financial reason to reduce the number

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:39.340
of people approved for benefits. The policy applies nationwide and affects how state budgets handle

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:45.600
enrollment. States must now pay a bigger portion of the benefits themselves rather than relying on

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.400
the previous federal matching arrangement. The shift began after the bill passed and is already

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:57.480
showing up in state fiscal planning documents. The economist reports that the funding shift leaves

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:03.620
states with growing bills and a built-in motive to lower participation. State officials must now

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:09.140
balance their own budgets against the same program rules that previously drew more federal dollars.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.240
Federal lawmakers who backed the measure argue it encourages efficiency and prevents long-term

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:21.180
dependence. Critics at the state level note that administrative costs rise even as federal

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:27.420
support shrinks, leaving less room for outreach or error correction. The reporting highlights that

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.140
enrollment decisions once funded mostly at the federal level now carry immediate state-level budget

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:39.840
consequences. One position holds that tying more costs to state budgets will prompt tighter eligibility

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.720
checks and reduce waste because local administrators see the direct fiscal impact and can adjust enrollment

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:53.320
practices accordingly. The opposing view maintains that the same incentive structure risks denying aid

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:59.520
to eligible households when states face revenue shortfalls, since the penalty for over-enrollment

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.440
now lands squarely on state treasuries rather than being shared with the federal government.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.920
Both arguments rest on the undisputed fact that the federal contribution per recipient has declined

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.520
under the new formula. What happens next? Enrollment decisions will continue to reflect these new state

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:24.000
level budget pressures and observers can track whether caseload changes align with economic indicators

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.840
or with new verification procedures. Shifting now to France, authorities ordered the removal of

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.640
